Given \( n+1 \) distinct values of \( x \) such that the polynomials
$$
\begin{array}{l}
f(x)=a_{n} x^{n}+a_{n-1} x^{n-1}+\cdots+a_{1} x+a_{0} \\
g(x)=b_{n} x^{n}+b_{n-1} x^{n-1}+\cdots+b_{1} x+b_{0}
\end{array}
$$
have equal values, then \( f(x) = g(x) \).

If $\frac{x}{y}=2$, then $\frac{x-y}{x}$ is equal to:
(a) -1 ;
(b) $-\frac{1}{2}$;
(c) $\frac{1}{2}$;
(d) 1 ;
(e) 2 ;

Let \[f(n) =
\begin{cases}
n^2-1 & \text{ if }n < 4,
\\ 3n-2 & \text{ if }n \geq 4.
\end{cases}
\]Find $f(f(f(2)))$.

Given that

\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{x}+\frac{1}{y}&=3,\\
xy+x+y&=4,
\end{align*}

compute $x^2y+xy^2$.

Compare the following statements: 
1) No three points on a circle lie on a straight line; 
2) A line cannot have more than two common points with a circle.

Let \[f(x) = \left\{
\begin{array}{cl}
x^2+1 &\text{ if }x>5, \\
2x-3 &\text{ if } -5 \le x \le 5, \\
3 &\text{ if } x <-5.
